  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/b09f63373bba1bb796f1832a4c7aa33ec220186e75bb84ccc79dcc46b662636e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-12).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-12),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-12$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
